<p class="small">The "worst case" is Digital Foundry's stress test with a grenade launcher on vehicles. They say that is not representative of normal play. Source: <a href={DF} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Digital Foundry</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">Why is there no 60fps mode?</h2>
<p>Rockstar's technology production director Marc Chambers told Kotaku the base presentation uses ray-traced reflections and global illumination, and that on PS5 Pro it adds ray-traced shadows and PSSR for clarity. His reason for the frame rate: the Pro's graphics chip and upscaler are upgraded, but <i>"the PS5 Pro's central processor is slightly better than the PS5 base console, it is not powerful enough to run the game's complex simulation at higher frame rates."</i> Source: <a href="https://www.polygon.com/gta-6-30-fps-locked-ps5-pro-performance/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Polygon</a>. We covered it when it broke in <a href="/news/gta-6-no-60fps-ps5-pro-rockstar-cpu-not-powerful-enough/">our story</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">The settings that actually matter</h2>
<p>Digital Foundry calls motion blur "one of the few settings in the game." Here's what you can and can't change on PS5.</p>
<h3>Motion Blur Strength: the one to try</h3>
<p>Pause, go to Settings, then the Display tab (Engadget says use the right shoulder button to get there). Under the visual effects heading you'll find <b>Motion Blur Strength</b>, a slider that defaults to <b>3 out of 10</b>. Set it to 0 to turn blur off. Digital Foundry says higher settings look smoother and lower settings look clearer, and Engadget notes that at lower frame rates a little blur can smooth out jittery camera moves, so try a notch or two before you go to zero. If blur makes you queasy, <a href="/gta-6/controls-accessibility/">the accessibility options</a> include more ways to calm the screen down. Sources: <a href="https://www.engadget.com/2321944/gta-6-how-to-adjust-turn-off-blur-motion/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Engadget</a>, <a href={DF} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Digital Foundry</a>.</p>
<h3>Vignetting: no slider, so don't go hunting</h3>
<p>Digital Foundry says other post-processing, like depth of field and vignetting, can't be adjusted. It adds that Rockstar says it recently identified an issue that made the vignette look stronger than intended. Rockstar's November 23 patch notes list crash, weather, autosave and Wanted Level fixes, and don't mention vignetting or performance. Sources: <a href={DF} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Digital Foundry</a>, <a href="https://support.rockstargames.com/articles/sN5pEdFYZdlvijKXd6xpJ/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rockstar Support</a>.</p>
<h3>VRR and 120Hz on PlayStation: leave them alone</h3>
<p>Digital Foundry says PlayStation has no 120Hz option, and that while you can force VRR on, the PS5's system-level VRR only works above 48fps and has no low frame-rate compensation. In a 30fps game, that means it does nothing. Xbox is different: consoles there can run the game in a 120Hz VRR container, which DF says gives a slightly smoother experience.</p>
<h3>Screen comfort options</h3>
<p>Rockstar's accessibility list includes adjustments for how intense the low-health, hit and police-chase screen effects are, camera shake, bobbing and sway adjustments, a high-contrast mode and a brightness reduction filter. We break those down on the <a href="/gta-6/controls-accessibility/">controls and accessibility page</a>. Source: <a href="https://support.rockstargames.com/articles/5g5CmDTne3WcpK7nsWsMR8/grand-theft-auto-vi-accessibility-features" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Rockstar Support</a>.</p>

<h2 class="sec">So which console should you play on?</h2>
<p>Digital Foundry's verdict: the PS5 Pro is the best option, with solid frame rates, 4K-like image quality and a visual upgrade from the ray-traced shadows. The game "looks and plays well" on base PS5 and Series X too, with occasional minor drops and a softer 1440p image. Series S is the hardest to recommend, with frame rates dropping into the mid-20s and a long list of visual cutbacks.</p>

<h2 class="sec">What about PC?</h2>
<p>Rockstar hasn't announced a PC version of GTA 6. That means there are no PC graphics settings to tune, and you can skip any "best PC settings" guide you run into. This page covers PS5 and Xbox only.</p>

